# Korean translation of TinyMUX server messages (#1419).
#
# Conformance locale.  Korean was chosen over Spanish deliberately: it is SOV
# with postpositions, so it reorders arguments where an SVO language would not,
# and reordering is the one property the `xx` pseudo-locale cannot test.  See
# the analysis on #1419.
#
# This catalogue is partial by design -- it covers the messages that exercise
# the runtime contract plus the common player-facing set.  Untranslated msgids
# fall back to English, which is the normal gettext behaviour.
#
# NOT NATIVE-REVIEWED.  The Korean here is intended to be structurally correct
# (particles, argument order, counter placement) so that it exercises the
# runtime honestly.  A native speaker should review the wording before this is
# offered to players.
#
# This file is distributed under the same license as the TinyMUX package.
#
# 메일 항목: "MAIL:" 접두사는 의도적으로 번역하지 않았습니다.  이는 산문이
# 아니라 하위 시스템 태그이며, 영어로 유지되는 @mail 명령어 이름 및 이미
# 그대로 두는 "@icmd:"와 같은 방식입니다.
#
#
# Extended (#1419, second tranche): the moment-to-moment player loop --
# movement, taking and dropping, giving, speech, paging, looking and the
# common refusals.  Still partial and still not native-reviewed.
#
# Two conventions worth a reviewer's attention, because they were CHANGED
# rather than merely added:
#
#   * Speech was "%s님이 말합니다, “%s”" -- verb first, then the quote, which
#     mirrors English word order.  It is now "%s님이 “%s”(이)라고 말합니다.",
#     the ordinary Korean quotative.  If the original order was deliberate
#     (to keep the quote at the end of the line for a client that truncates,
#     say), revert it and leave a note -- it was not obvious either way.
#
#   * The quotative particle needs 이라고 after a consonant and 라고 after a
#     vowel, and the quoted text is a %s whose ending cannot be known.  It is
#     written "(이)라고", matching how this file already handles 을(를),
#     이(가) and (으)로 for the same reason.
#
# Giving and counting reorder with %N$ because Korean puts the recipient
# before the object and the counter after the noun.  That is the property
# this catalogue exists to exercise (tests/nls/run_ko.sh case 4).
#
#
